WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:13.640
- So welcome everyone to
the second Shulman Lecture

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.640
in the Humanities for Metaphysics
Meets Cognitive Science.

00:00:18.974 --> 00:00:23.974
Today I'm super-pleased to
welcome philosophy's rockstar

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.780
and that would be Professor David Chalmers

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.640
who is a university
Professor of Philosophy

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.123
and Neuroscience at NYU.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.540
So Dave is a philosopher

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.800
but also did his PhD in
Philosophy and Cognitive Science

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.600
at Indiana University with Doug Hofstadter

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.040
and he also does a lot
of collaborative work

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.380
with neuroscientists and cognoscientists.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.223
So he fits right in with our venue.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:56.170
So I mean Dave is a
rockstar, partly just, well.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.290
In all ways shall we say.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.240
So his work is obviously that way.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.120
He's known for, both I think
advancing really interesting,

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.685
speculative, controversial, new ideas

00:01:12.685 --> 00:01:15.140
and also he has, one of the
things I love about his work

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.810
is he has an eye

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.800
for understanding deep
philosophical questions

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.040
and how to frame them,

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.650
both in distinctive new ways

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.810
and also in ways that kind of even,

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.830
like conventional philosophers
can grasp and understand

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.670
and engage with.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.670
He's also looks like a
rockstar which I really love.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.510
He's got the hair and
when I first met Dave,

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.320
he had even longer hair.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.360
I was an impressionable, very
stressed out grad student

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.120
and in an American Philosophical
Association meeting,

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.180
Dave was in what was
called back in the day

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.940
The Smoker which is this horrible event

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:49.870
which I will go on with you later

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.620
but for all of us that
were graduate students,

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.690
we were all traumatized and Dave,

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:55.110
one of the outstanding things about him

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.190
is that he's incredibly welcoming

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:00.030
and supportive of new people in the field

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.620
and engages in really productive
conversations with them

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:03.860
and he did that for me

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.070
and it was probably the first time

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.470
I'd ever had that happen
and I felt so welcomed.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:10.280
That's my primary memory of
Dave but that has continued.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:11.320
I've known him for a long time

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.250
and he has always been that way with me,

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.320
with other philosophers kind of,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.320
who are kind of getting
into the field and also in,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:19.750
obviously with more senior people

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.740
and people who are kind of full-on engaged

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.820
with all of the major topics in the area.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:27.462
So he's really a leader in all ways.

00:02:27.462 --> 00:02:32.130
And I guess I will not
keep going on but I could.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.710
Let's just welcome Dave.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:34.543
He's gonna speak to us

00:02:34.543 --> 00:02:36.580
about "The Meta-Problem of Consciousness".

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:37.413
Thank you.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.800
- Well such a pleasure to
be here with old friends

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:49.800
and new talking in this series.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.380
I'm gonna be talking about,

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.460
well as Lori mentioned
I went to my advisor

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:01.490
in graduate school in
philosophy and cognitive science

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.410
at Indiana University
about 30 years ago now,

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:08.250
was Douglas Hofstadter.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.340
I don't know how many of you
have read Hofstadter's works

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.790
like "Godel, Escher, Bach" and so on

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:18.500
but he was particularly
fond of the meta perspective

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.860
to almost any problem.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.800
I mean there's this slogan

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.767
which is sometimes attributed to him.

00:03:23.767 --> 00:03:27.140
"Anything you can do, I can do meta."

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.030
He denies that it was him

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:30.840
and there's actually a long trail

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.770
of interesting history
for that very slogan

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:35.650
that I won't go into.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:36.680
If you go online actually,

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.200
you can find a meme attributing
this to Immanuel Kant

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.143
which is not especially plausible.

00:03:43.047 --> 00:03:48.047
But yeah, so the idea today is

00:03:48.874 --> 00:03:49.930
that thinking about making progress

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.300
on the problem of consciousness.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.300
Maybe one form of progress
can be achieved by going meta.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.540
By jumping out of the
system, going up a level

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:02.100
and that's gonna be what I'm going to

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:05.123
try to explore in what follows.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.113
Okay, so when I was.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.480
Actually in graduate
school I went to a talk

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.480
where they told me that meta
X always means X about X.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:26.100
So metacognition,
cognition about cognition.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.990
A metatheory, it's a
theory about the theory.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:31.310
Okay, of course we immediately set about

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:34.950
finding counter examples and metaphysics.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.640
Is that physics about physics?

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:37.733
No, not really.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:40.770
Meta ethics, is that ethics about ethics?

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:41.603
No, not really.

00:04:41.603 --> 00:04:44.563
But this one actually fits the template.

00:04:44.563 --> 00:04:48.450
The meta-problem of
consciousness is so-called

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:51.393
because it's a problem about a problem.

00:04:52.578 --> 00:04:55.230
And the meta-problem of
consciousness as I understand it

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.953
is to explain why we think and say

00:04:58.953 --> 00:05:01.457
that there's a problem of consciousness

00:05:01.457 --> 00:05:04.070
and my thought is that
that's a distinctive approach

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.130
to the problem of consciousness

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:09.900
that might itself be
productive and interesting.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.480
I've been told I need to
go exactly 45 minutes,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.263
so let me make sure I've got
my timer in hand, very good.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:20.750
Lori is fully authorized
to cut me off at 45 minutes

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:21.963
and zero seconds.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.333
I need that stopwatch
with a second hand now.

00:05:26.188 --> 00:05:30.300
Okay, so the meta problem is
a problem about a problem.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:34.350
That presupposes first order
problem about consciousness

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:35.770
and in this context,

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:40.653
that problem is the so-called
hard problem of consciousness.

00:05:41.815 --> 00:05:45.410
I think we said I was going
to take my mask off right?

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:47.360
If that's, I gather the convention

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:49.840
is for speakers to remove the mask

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.030
and audience to keep it on.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.910
If anybody is uncomfortable
and prefer I put it back on,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:54.760
just let me know.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:05:59.820
The hard problem of consciousness

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:03.490
is the problem of explaining why and how

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:08.263
physical processes give rise
to conscious experience.

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:12.540
Here, okay so here's an illustration

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.290
of the hard problem which I quite like

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:16.850
because it somehow
illustrates consciousness

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:19.830
as someone's hair catching fire.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:21.500
This is maybe some kind of illustration

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:26.040
of the magic of conscious experience

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.040
but the hard problem is
concerned with what philosophers

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.130
often call phenomenal consciousness

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:36.940
which is basically tied to
what it's like to be a subject.

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:40.240
So you know, this locution goes back

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:45.240
at least to a famous paper by
my NYU colleague Tom Nagel.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:49.687
In 1974 he wrote, "What
is it like to be a bat?"

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.090
And the thought was although we don't know

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:54.530
what it's like to be a bat.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:57.420
So engaging in sonar,
is it more like vision,

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.100
is it more like hearing?

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:02.550
But presumably there's
something it's like to be a bat.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:05.370
A bat has subjective experience.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.030
Just as there's something
it's like to be me,

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:08.910
there's something it's like to be you.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:11.550
We assume something it's like to be a bat,

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.910
most people would say
nothing it's like to be say,

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:17.093
a cup or a water bottle.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.810
There's something it's
like to be a system,

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:22.690
you know roughly when it has

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:25.090
some kind of subjective experience.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:26.960
So we say a system is
phenomenally conscious

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.120
if there's something it's like to be it.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.013
A mental state is phenomenally conscious

00:07:32.013 --> 00:07:35.320
if there's something it's
like to be in that state.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.660
So states of phenomenal
consciousness include

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:43.630
visual experiences like the
experience of color and depth.

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:46.430
There's something it's like
to have those experiences.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:50.800
Other sensory experiences
like of sound or taste.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:54.690
Bodily sensations like pain or orgasm.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:58.370
Mental imagery like recalled visual images

00:07:58.370 --> 00:07:59.713
from your childhood.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:04.650
Emotional experiences
like happiness or anger

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.920
or indeed a stream of a current thought

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:09.020
like reflection and decision.

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:10.550
All sometimes thought of as elements

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:13.480
of this rich inner movie of consciousness.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:17.003
States that feel like
something to the subject.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:20.930
The hard, the locution the hard problem

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:24.550
is meant to contrast with other problems

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:28.510
of consciousness and of
cognition more generally.

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:30.530
The so-called easy problems

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:32.520
which aren't easy in any absolute sense.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.890
They're as hard as any problem in science

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:36.740
but they're called the easy problem

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:39.510
because we've got some,
at least, better grip

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.810
on how to go about explaining them.

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:46.090
These are basically problems
of explaining behavioral

00:08:46.090 --> 00:08:51.090
or cognitive functions like
say perceptual discrimination,

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:54.650
the integration of
information in the brain,

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:57.190
the control of behavior

00:08:57.190 --> 00:09:00.847
and indeed verbal reports like saying,

00:09:00.847 --> 00:09:03.200
"I see that object now."

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.970
These are all relatively
objective features

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:08.223
of the human system.

00:09:09.553 --> 00:09:13.060
We have a fairly standard
paradigm for explaining them

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.160
in cognitive science.

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:16.930
Roughly we handle the easy problems,

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:18.894
these problems of explaining behavioral

00:09:18.894 --> 00:09:22.700
or cognitive functions by finding a neural

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:24.310
or a computational mechanism

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:27.503
that performs the relevant function.

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:30.410
Maybe a neural system,

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:32.960
maybe some kind of algorithmic mechanism

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.760
but once you've explained how it is

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.490
that that function is performed,

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:39.990
you've explained the problem.

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.560
But for the hard problem of
consciousness distinctively,

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:43.580
it looks like explaining

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:47.600
all those objective
behavioral cognitive functions

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:51.070
seems to always leave
open a further question.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:54.860
Why is all that accompanied by experience?

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:59.110
Why is this something it's like
to undergo those processes?

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:03.200
You know why doesn't all that
processing go on in the dark,

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:06.014
without consciousness as it might in say,

00:10:06.014 --> 00:10:07.714
I don't know, a robot or a zombie?

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.180
There seems to be what philosophers call

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:12.970
an explanatory gap here

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:17.860
between physical processes
and subjective experience.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:19.790
So that's the hard problem.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.420
Here's an illustration

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:22.730
of something very strange happening

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:25.490
in the vicinity of the
brain and consciousness

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.850
that we're trying to get to the bottom of

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:30.550
in solving the hard problem.

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:34.560
And there are many different
approaches to the hard problem.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:35.440
In both,

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:38.900
coming both from the side of
metaphysics and philosophy

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.960
or from the side of the
science of consciousness.

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:43.670
I mean there are approaches that take

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:47.470
consciousness to be irreducible in nature

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:48.450
and this is actually the side

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:50.747
which I have somewhat more sympathy for

00:10:50.747 --> 00:10:53.190
and I've argued myself
in some earlier work,

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:56.720
that consciousness could be
fundamental and irreducible

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.970
in the same way that
space and time and mass

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:01.610
are often taken to be.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:03.590
This leads you to views like dualism,

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:06.890
consciousness is distinct
from any physical properties

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:09.553
but is somehow in interaction with them.

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:14.260
Or a panpsychism, that there's
some element of consciousness

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:16.563
at the base of all physical systems.

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:18.790
Or even idealism,

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:23.720
that the whole world is itself
a construct of consciousness.

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:26.070
Okay so that's one set of options.

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:30.693
Another set of options is that
consciousness is reducible.

00:11:31.997 --> 00:11:36.532
Perhaps to some kind of
computational or functional process

00:11:36.532 --> 00:11:40.100
as popular functionalist views hold.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.849
Perhaps to something really
distinctively biological,

00:11:42.849 --> 00:11:44.930
that's biological materialism.

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:46.390
Some people have even speculated

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:49.020
maybe there's something
essentially quantum mechanical

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:50.445
about consciousness

00:11:50.445 --> 00:11:53.730
and we can reduce it to some
kind of quantum materialism

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:56.338
and there's been a big debate about these,

00:11:56.338 --> 00:11:58.720
about these options here.

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:02.800
In both the metaphysics and
the science of consciousness

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:05.800
but here I'm not gonna be
concerned with either of those.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.490
Instead I'm gonna be
taking a third approach.

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:11.720
The third approach is to do what yeah,

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:14.570
what Douglas Hofstadter called
jumping out of the system

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:16.530
and going meta.

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:19.987
So instead of trying to solve
the problem we ask instead,

00:12:19.987 --> 00:12:22.327
"Why is it that we think

00:12:22.327 --> 00:12:25.770
"that consciousness poses a problem?"

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:27.430
And when you ask the question in this way,

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:30.430
I think it just gives you
just a different angle

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:31.263
on the problem

00:12:31.263 --> 00:12:34.853
that comes with all kinds
of promising new directions.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:39.640
One way to get at this

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:41.830
is to look at various behavioral functions

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:45.600
that are closely related
to the hard problem.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.280
The hard problem's about
experience, not about behavior

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:50.722
but there are behavioral functions

00:12:50.722 --> 00:12:53.120
that are very closely connected.

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:55.120
Not least that we make what I'll call,

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:59.610
phenomenal reports or reports
of conscious experience.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.360
Oh dear, I hope that's not ominous.

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:05.023
Ah yeah, perfect.

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:08.980
Okay so yeah these objective function

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:11.110
which is closely related to consciousness

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.390
is the existence of phenomenal reports

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:16.180
or verbal reports of
conscious experiences.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:17.657
People say things like,

00:13:17.657 --> 00:13:20.437
"I am conscious, there's
something it's like to be me.

00:13:20.437 --> 00:13:23.830
"I am feeling pain now and so on."

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:26.570
And although consciousness
itself may be a hard problem,

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:28.560
looks like these reports
are in the vicinity

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:29.910
of objective functions,

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:34.000
potentially explicable by
the standard mechanisms

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:35.960
of cognitive science.

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:39.020
So in principle, explaining
those falls under the category

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:40.033
of the easy problem.

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:42.750
Oh sorry, next one.

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:49.720
Then we also have what
I call problem reports

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:50.650
which are you know,

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:54.980
reports that consciousness
seems like peculiarly mysterious

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:57.860
or difficult to explain.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:13:58.900
Sorry, can we go back one?

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:00.820
To the one that says problem reports.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:01.653
Perfect.

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:04.290
Reports expressing our sense

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:06.660
that consciousness poses a hard problem.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:08.700
Maybe it's a slightly smaller group now

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:10.580
but there are at least
some people out there

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:11.497
who say things like,

00:14:11.497 --> 00:14:13.952
"Boy it's really
disturbing seeing yourself

00:14:13.952 --> 00:14:15.677
"like that from the side."

00:14:17.868 --> 00:14:20.080
But yeah, right yeah.

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:23.730
Super meta, self consciousness
upon self consciousness

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:27.123
and who is that zombie David
Chalmers who's underneath?

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:30.160
That's really worrying.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:36.440
There is a hard problem of consciousness,

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:38.818
people say things like explaining behavior

00:14:38.818 --> 00:14:41.013
doesn't explain consciousness.

00:14:42.607 --> 00:14:45.150
Oh fantastic, thanks.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:46.000
Sorry about that.

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:50.630
Consciousness seems
nonphysical and so on again.

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:52.500
Those things are behaviors,

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:54.880
in principle open to paradigmatic,

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:56.830
cognitive science explanation.

00:14:56.830 --> 00:15:00.240
So the meta problem of
consciousness as I understand it

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:03.310
is basically the problem of explaining

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:06.030
those problem reports.

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.180
In principle, puzzle about behavior.

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:10.080
So it's an easy problem

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.823
or to be open to standard kinds
of functional explanation.

00:15:16.056 --> 00:15:18.530
So the meta problem is an easy problem

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:21.690
but it's closely tied to the hard problem.

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:22.960
In some ways it looks more tractable

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:24.040
than the hard problem

00:15:25.087 --> 00:15:26.370
and solving ought to at
least shed some light

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.932
on the hard problem.

00:15:28.932 --> 00:15:30.440
If you like, this is
a kind of potentially,

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:33.364
what people call a genealogical approach

00:15:33.364 --> 00:15:35.982
to philosophical problems.

00:15:35.982 --> 00:15:38.466
We don't exactly try and
solve the problem directly

00:15:38.466 --> 00:15:40.820
but we look at why it is
that this problem exists

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:42.423
in the first place.

00:15:42.423 --> 00:15:44.933
Sometimes people do this
by focusing on cultural,

00:15:44.933 --> 00:15:47.100
on cultural history

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:50.285
or maybe some kind of
evolutionary history.

00:15:50.285 --> 00:15:51.500
For example, you know, you might take,

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:54.020
some people take this approach to God.

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:56.150
Instead of trying to
figure out is there a God,

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:57.830
figure out why do we think there's a God

00:15:57.830 --> 00:15:59.430
in the first place.

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:01.510
Sometimes if you find some evolutionary

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:05.360
or cultural explanation of
why we think there's a God,

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:07.750
some people then think
okay, that actually debunks

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:09.784
the existence of God.

00:16:09.784 --> 00:16:13.711
We no longer have strong
reason to believe in God

00:16:13.711 --> 00:16:16.843
after we have explained
why we have these beliefs.

00:16:16.843 --> 00:16:17.841
That much is controversial

00:16:17.841 --> 00:16:20.000
but it's quite common to take this line

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:23.339
about consciousness at
least in recent years.

00:16:23.339 --> 00:16:26.044
A few people have taken it

00:16:26.044 --> 00:16:29.422
and this leads to the view
sometimes called illusionism.

00:16:29.422 --> 00:16:33.180
That consciousness or at least
the problem of consciousness

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:35.113
is an illusion.

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:37.033
We believe that there are these special,

00:16:37.033 --> 00:16:40.040
we have these special
properties that pose a problem

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:44.020
but we don't really explain
why we have those beliefs

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:46.083
and we might dissolve the problem.

00:16:47.261 --> 00:16:49.480
So that's to say that
solving the meta problem

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:52.053
could dissolve the hard problem.

00:16:54.518 --> 00:16:55.960
And in recent years that's actually become

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:58.342
quite a popular view.

00:16:58.342 --> 00:17:00.746
This started to receive
some systematic attention.

00:17:00.746 --> 00:17:05.390
Here's a book that
focused on a central paper

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:06.887
by Keith Frankish called

00:17:06.887 --> 00:17:09.781
"Illusionism: As the
Theory of Consciousness".

00:17:09.781 --> 00:17:11.642
Yeah, depicting the mind-body problem

00:17:11.642 --> 00:17:13.006
as some kind of magic trick.

00:17:13.006 --> 00:17:14.005
We all seem to ourselves

00:17:14.005 --> 00:17:17.550
to have these magical properties
that in fact, we don't.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:22.550
In fact illusionism has a
long history in philosophy.

00:17:23.403 --> 00:17:26.457
So Immanuel Kant in the
Critique of Pure Reason

00:17:26.457 --> 00:17:29.365
talked about some elements
of the unity of self

00:17:29.365 --> 00:17:30.933
as a transcendental illusion.

00:17:31.855 --> 00:17:36.087
The materialist philosopher U. T. Place

00:17:36.087 --> 00:17:37.287
who identified mental states
for the brain states said,

00:17:37.287 --> 00:17:39.890
"Yeah we have this sense
there's something more."

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:42.931
But he said it was a kind
of phenomenological fallacy.

00:17:42.931 --> 00:17:46.350
Another Australian
philosopher, David Armstrong

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:49.230
talked about a distinctive
kind of illusion

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:52.022
tied to a circus illusion

00:17:52.022 --> 00:17:53.410
called the headless woman illusion.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:54.823
I'll get to that shortly.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:59.870
More recently Dan Dennett has talked about

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:03.693
a consciousness as a user illusion.

00:18:04.701 --> 00:18:05.840
Keith Frankish I mentioned already.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:07.810
Michael Graziano, the psychologist

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:11.030
had something he calls the
attention schema theory.

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:13.980
That we have all these schemas
for thinking about the mind.

00:18:14.879 --> 00:18:16.529
Self models that drive us astray.

00:18:17.964 --> 00:18:21.023
So it's a, it's a popular
and important view.

00:18:21.023 --> 00:18:23.305
I don't think though,
so many illusionists.

00:18:23.305 --> 00:18:24.550
Illusionists have a special motivation

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:27.490
to be interested in the
meta problem but I think,

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:29.702
I'm not myself an illusionist.

00:18:29.702 --> 00:18:33.550
I think the meta problem is
interesting even independently

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:36.680
of being an illusionist.

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:39.960
I'm what philosophers call a
realist about consciousness.

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:42.070
In philosophy, to a first approximation,

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:43.910
to be a realist about something

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:45.960
is roughly to believe it's real.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:49.900
So a moral realist is someone
who thinks morality is real.

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:53.750
A realist about consciousness
thinks consciousness is real.

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:55.813
I think consciousness is not an illusion.

00:18:56.805 --> 00:18:58.187
I think that solving,

00:18:58.187 --> 00:18:59.296
I don't think that
solving the meta problem

00:18:59.296 --> 00:19:01.830
suddenly dissolves the hard problem.

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:02.860
But nonetheless,

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:06.023
I think the meta problem is
interesting in its own right.

00:19:07.724 --> 00:19:09.100
I think it's tractable and that solving it

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:11.100
has the potential to at the very least,

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:14.603
shed much light on the hard problem,

00:19:14.603 --> 00:19:17.503
even if it doesn't constitute
a solution in its own right.

00:19:19.543 --> 00:19:20.463
When I think there's actually a,

00:19:21.424 --> 00:19:23.923
there's actually a research program here.

00:19:23.923 --> 00:19:26.463
What I'll call the
meta-problem research program

00:19:26.463 --> 00:19:29.046
which I think is a potentially
very productive one.

00:19:29.046 --> 00:19:32.007
It's open to realists
and illusionists alike

00:19:32.007 --> 00:19:33.559
to try and solve the meta-problem.

00:19:33.559 --> 00:19:36.433
Try and explain these problem reports.

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:40.340
It's open to reductionists
and non-reductionists,

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:43.361
illusionists and non-illusionists.

00:19:43.361 --> 00:19:44.520
We can try and solve the meta problem

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:47.644
and then think about the
philosophical consequences.

00:19:47.644 --> 00:19:50.750
One reason why I think this
meta-problem program is cool

00:19:50.750 --> 00:19:55.170
is it comes with a fairly clear program

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:59.784
in both philosophy and cognitive science.

00:19:59.784 --> 00:20:00.850
Remember the problem was basically,

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:02.430
really we might put this as to explain

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:05.263
certain intuitions we have.

00:20:05.263 --> 00:20:06.685
It's really to explain certain reports

00:20:06.685 --> 00:20:10.563
but maybe some underlying intuitions

00:20:10.563 --> 00:20:12.510
or maybe to explain why
it's possible to explain

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:15.083
these intuitions.

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:21.550
But yeah, this actually brings in

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:23.810
I think a number of different areas

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:27.111
of cognitive science really at the core.

00:20:27.111 --> 00:20:29.340
It's a problem, the
problem of consciousness.

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:32.900
You might think it comes from
a philosophical background

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:34.343
as the mind-body problem.

00:20:35.278 --> 00:20:37.150
A problem in metaphysics, what
is the nature of the mind?

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:39.156
The nature of consciousness.

00:20:39.156 --> 00:20:40.880
But by focusing on the meta-problem,

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:43.700
this at least carves
off a bit of the problem

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:47.993
that comes with a tractable
program in cognitive science.

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:51.538
So one thing we want to do I think first

00:20:51.538 --> 00:20:54.350
is to distinguish a bunch
of different problem reports

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:58.640
or problem intuitions
that people often display

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:00.730
that lead people to think there's a,

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:02.340
there's a, that are associated

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:05.170
with there being a
problem of consciousness

00:21:06.076 --> 00:21:08.093
and I divide these into
at least four groups.

00:21:09.036 --> 00:21:11.273
There are metaphysical intuitions,

00:21:12.361 --> 00:21:13.860
intuitions to the effect
that consciousness seems

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:16.093
somehow to be non-physical.

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:20.310
There are explanatory intuitions

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:22.560
tied to this core idea that consciousness

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.240
is hard to explain,

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:26.200
explaining these objective functions

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:27.843
doesn't explain consciousness.

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:31.317
There are knowledge intuitions.

00:21:31.317 --> 00:21:32.230
Here the paradigmatic one would be

00:21:32.230 --> 00:21:34.890
Frank Jackson's classic case of Mary

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:38.410
the scientist who was brought
up in a black and white room,

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:41.060
knows all the physical
facts about color processing

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:44.605
in the brain and
wavelengths and associations

00:21:44.605 --> 00:21:48.430
but it seems she doesn't know
one really important thing,

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:50.873
she doesn't know what
it's like to see red.

00:21:51.798 --> 00:21:53.970
One day she sees a red
rose for the first time

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:56.877
and she learns something new.

00:21:56.877 --> 00:21:59.298
So that's the knowledge intuition.

00:21:59.298 --> 00:22:00.470
Then there are, yeah
there are modal intuitions

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:02.713
tied to what we can imagine or conceive.

00:22:03.573 --> 00:22:04.833
What seems possible.

00:22:04.833 --> 00:22:06.854
So you know, various
philosophers including me

00:22:06.854 --> 00:22:09.760
have argued we can at
least conceive of zombies,

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:13.839
systems functionally
perhaps physically like us

00:22:13.839 --> 00:22:15.755
but without consciousness.

00:22:15.755 --> 00:22:18.823
So these are some of the
core intuitions in the,

00:22:20.053 --> 00:22:22.680
associated with the
problem of consciousness.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:23.670
There are some others too.

00:22:23.670 --> 00:22:26.030
Maybe slightly less close to the core.

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:28.439
There are intuitions about

00:22:28.439 --> 00:22:29.730
the distribution of consciousness.

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:32.351
Maybe that robots are not conscious

00:22:32.351 --> 00:22:35.416
or that groups are not
conscious for example.

00:22:35.416 --> 00:22:37.840
Intuitions about the
value of consciousness,

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:39.970
that consciousness matters morally.

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:42.400
Actually yesterday I was giving a talk

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:45.663
which turned in central part
on that kind of intuition.

00:22:47.702 --> 00:22:50.416
Intuitions about the self,

00:22:50.416 --> 00:22:51.630
that the self persists through time.

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:54.938
Intuitions about the qualities
presented in consciousness.

00:22:54.938 --> 00:22:56.950
That special quality,
like the redness of red

00:22:56.950 --> 00:23:00.300
is somehow directly available to me.

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:03.133
But I'll concentrate
especially on the first four.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:07.220
Now I think one of the
cool things about this

00:23:07.220 --> 00:23:10.610
meta-problem project is it comes with

00:23:11.459 --> 00:23:14.437
a very natural interdisciplinary
research program.

00:23:14.437 --> 00:23:17.619
All kinds of work is
potentially relevant here.

00:23:17.619 --> 00:23:20.900
There's experimental work in
both experimental psychology

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:22.500
and in let's call it these days,

00:23:23.336 --> 00:23:25.660
experimental philosophy
where you empirically study

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:28.250
people's intuitions about these things.

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.760
Here, particularly studying
people's intuitions

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:35.200
about consciousness and
the associated problems.

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:39.150
There's also building of
models, computational models,

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:43.650
neurobiological models
of intuitions and reports

00:23:44.519 --> 00:23:45.352
and there's also a whole lot of room

00:23:45.352 --> 00:23:47.443
for philosophical analysis.

00:23:48.379 --> 00:23:50.230
So I think this meta-problem program

00:23:50.230 --> 00:23:53.950
is just ripe for
interdisciplinary exploration

00:23:54.853 --> 00:23:58.358
and it's been getting a bit
of that in recent years.

00:23:58.358 --> 00:23:59.191
I mean for starters,

00:23:59.191 --> 00:24:00.600
I mean it's an empirical question

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:04.253
how widely these intuitions are shared.

00:24:06.015 --> 00:24:10.940
In a paper I wrote on this maybe,

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:12.690
four years or so ago now I argued

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:15.193
they were gonna be very widely shared

00:24:15.193 --> 00:24:18.023
and you'd expect them
to be and not universal.

00:24:18.864 --> 00:24:20.807
Some people argued with that

00:24:20.807 --> 00:24:21.940
and there actually begun to be

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:23.931
some empirical studies of this.

00:24:23.931 --> 00:24:25.563
My own sense is that,

00:24:26.779 --> 00:24:31.070
yeah and these intuitions,
one might expect

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:33.058
to be quite widely shared.

00:24:33.058 --> 00:24:34.815
Especially if one's a
realist about consciousness,

00:24:34.815 --> 00:24:36.730
you might think well the
source of these intuitions

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:39.360
is gotta be deeply tied to
actually being conscious.

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:43.099
Presumably being conscious is
something that's universal,

00:24:43.099 --> 00:24:44.434
at least if one's a realist.

00:24:44.434 --> 00:24:46.510
So at least that source of the intuitions

00:24:46.510 --> 00:24:48.013
should be widely shared.

00:24:48.855 --> 00:24:50.016
Maybe it doesn't actually lead

00:24:50.016 --> 00:24:52.956
to these intuitions in
everybody but you might think,

00:24:52.956 --> 00:24:55.040
okay, at the very least a
tendency in that direction

00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:57.118
ought to be widely shared.

00:24:57.118 --> 00:24:59.513
A disposition to say these things.

00:25:00.375 --> 00:25:03.553
Even if it's sometimes
overridden by other things in a,

00:25:04.620 --> 00:25:06.574
in any given system.

00:25:06.574 --> 00:25:07.648
I mean the alternative view though

00:25:07.648 --> 00:25:09.450
is that they're somehow
cultural and local.

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:12.160
Maybe Descartes, Rene
Descartes gets blamed

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:15.560
for a lot of stuff in philosophy
and cognitive science.

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:20.074
Maybe these mind-body intuitions
are all Descartes' fault.

00:25:20.074 --> 00:25:21.520
I find that very deeply implausible myself

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:24.990
but then maybe I would,

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:28.760
given that I have these
intuitions very strongly

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:31.720
but I do think this is
an empirical question.

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:34.115
You know traditionally I think,

00:25:34.115 --> 00:25:35.495
and you know the last couple of decades

00:25:35.495 --> 00:25:36.328
of cognitive science,

00:25:36.328 --> 00:25:37.530
there's been a fair
amount of empirical work

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:40.630
on intuitions about the
mind but that were concerns,

00:25:40.630 --> 00:25:41.990
especially I think belief

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:45.640
like under what conditions
infants have the concept

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:46.823
of a false belief.

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:49.350
Intuitions about the self.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:52.890
Like how, how selves persist over time.

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:55.513
Whether they might continue
after death and so on.

00:25:56.515 --> 00:25:57.348
Where consciousness is concerned,

00:25:57.348 --> 00:25:58.530
perhaps the biggest body of work

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:02.220
has been on distribution
of consciousness in robots

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:04.852
and groups and so on.

00:26:04.852 --> 00:26:07.372
Josh Knobe has done some
well-known work about,

00:26:07.372 --> 00:26:08.499
on those questions.

00:26:08.499 --> 00:26:12.699
Where, where problem
intuitions are concerned.

00:26:12.699 --> 00:26:13.711
At least, when I wrote this,

00:26:13.711 --> 00:26:15.474
when I first started writing about this

00:26:15.474 --> 00:26:17.450
four or five years ago
there wasn't so much.

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:18.550
Now there's come to be,

00:26:18.550 --> 00:26:21.310
there's come to at least
be a small literature

00:26:21.310 --> 00:26:25.191
experimentally studying these intuitions.

00:26:25.191 --> 00:26:26.513
In something I wrote I said the one,

00:26:26.513 --> 00:26:27.900
the intuition I would
expect to be most robust

00:26:27.900 --> 00:26:31.410
and widespread is the intuition about Mary

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:33.280
in the black and white room.

00:26:33.280 --> 00:26:35.100
Not knowing what it's like to see red

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:36.900
and then learning something.

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:39.590
Just recently, Daniel Gregory

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:41.997
and a couple of other philosophers

00:26:41.997 --> 00:26:44.040
have published a very nice
experimental study of this

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:46.697
called "Who knows what Mary knew?"

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:51.010
And they find that,
they asked the question.

00:26:51.010 --> 00:26:53.537
Did Mary know what it's like to see red?

00:26:53.537 --> 00:26:54.780
When she had that complete
physical knowledge

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:58.180
but had not yet actually
experienced the color,

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.980
did Mary know what it's like to see red?

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:03.530
82% said no she didn't

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:05.855
but then upon seeing
it for the first time,

00:27:05.855 --> 00:27:06.740
did she then know what
it's like to see red?

00:27:06.740 --> 00:27:08.267
90% said yes.

00:27:09.158 --> 00:27:11.335
So okay that's fairly robust.

00:27:11.335 --> 00:27:13.430
I mean no one expects to get
100% on any of these things

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:18.393
but 80 to 90% indicates
some pretty serious effect.

00:27:19.430 --> 00:27:22.303
There are also explanatory intuitions.

00:27:23.530 --> 00:27:27.370
There's a nice paper by Sara
Gottlieb and Tania Lombrozo.

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:29.080
It doesn't focus just on consciousness

00:27:29.080 --> 00:27:34.080
but focuses on questions
about under what conditions

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:35.560
do people have the intuition

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:37.020
that certain mental phenomena

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:40.860
are hard to explain
scientifically and they find

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:42.410
people seem to think that mental phenomena

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:44.490
are especially hard to explain

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:47.300
when they're tied to subjective experience

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:50.136
and privileged awareness.

00:27:50.136 --> 00:27:52.830
Which at least goes
along with the idea that

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:55.500
some distinctive explanatory intuitions

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:57.360
tied to the hard problem.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:00.956
Tied to the hardness of the
hard problem for consciousness.

00:28:00.956 --> 00:28:04.190
More recently there's an
article by Rodrigo Diaz

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:08.180
called "Do people think
consciousness poses a hard problem"

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:10.210
where he's actually trying to
argue that these intuitions

00:28:10.210 --> 00:28:12.220
are not so widespread

00:28:13.077 --> 00:28:15.695
and actually there's a lot
to say about this paper

00:28:15.695 --> 00:28:18.238
which is very interesting.

00:28:18.238 --> 00:28:19.956
It turns out a whole lot depends on things

00:28:19.956 --> 00:28:23.733
like your preamble and your
setting up of the problem.

00:28:24.617 --> 00:28:25.880
If you've set it up
with super pro-science,

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:27.870
neurobiology ra ra,

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:30.120
then people say okay yeah
science is doing great.

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:31.340
If you set it up with a lot of stuff

00:28:31.340 --> 00:28:35.223
about how consciousness is
so mysterious, not so good.

00:28:36.071 --> 00:28:38.430
But with a reasonably neutral setup,

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:40.130
I think he gets about 50% of people saying

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:42.230
consciousness is hard to explain.

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:44.213
50% not.

00:28:45.095 --> 00:28:46.932
So interesting to know
what to make of this.

00:28:46.932 --> 00:28:48.990
So much to, I mean it's
hard to experimentally test

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:51.160
a lot of these things
because we have to use words

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:54.790
like explanation which is so
ambiguous and conceivability

00:28:54.790 --> 00:28:57.900
which has got technical
meanings and so on.

00:28:57.900 --> 00:28:59.640
So tricky but at least we're,

00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:02.460
I think the literature
here at least is beginning

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:04.277
to develop.

00:29:04.277 --> 00:29:05.275
Actually I've done

00:29:05.275 --> 00:29:08.240
a tiny bit of empirical
work about this myself.

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:10.410
I'm not exactly an
experimental philosopher

00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:14.500
but every now and then I've
taken empirical surveys

00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:17.429
of intuitions about philosophy.

00:29:17.429 --> 00:29:21.690
Restricted to the class of
professional philosophers.

00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:25.731
So David Bourget and I are independently,

00:29:25.731 --> 00:29:29.460
we co-developed a website, PhilPapers

00:29:29.460 --> 00:29:34.350
which is basically a giant
bibliography of philosophy.

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:39.350
Two million entries and
it's basically turned into

00:29:40.460 --> 00:29:45.001
a giant database of
philosophy of all kinds

00:29:45.001 --> 00:29:45.834
and well along the way
this involved building

00:29:45.834 --> 00:29:48.830
a giant database of email
addresses of all philosophers.

00:29:48.830 --> 00:29:50.780
So we found ourselves back in 2009

00:29:50.780 --> 00:29:53.410
in a position to contact
I think philosophers

00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:56.930
in 100 departments of
philosophy around the world

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:59.780
and to ask them for their views

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:03.540
on 30 key philosophical
problems like materialism.

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:08.540
Yes, no, other with various other options.

00:30:09.070 --> 00:30:12.280
We did it again in 2020,
this time with 100 questions

00:30:12.280 --> 00:30:13.970
and many more philosophers

00:30:14.808 --> 00:30:18.763
and I think we asked 8000
and got 2000 responses

00:30:18.763 --> 00:30:20.224
and one of the questions this time was,

00:30:20.224 --> 00:30:22.126
"Is there a hard problem
of consciousness?"

00:30:22.126 --> 00:30:24.930
And we found 62% of philosophers say yes.

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:27.263
30% say no.

00:30:27.263 --> 00:30:29.550
Okay this is a rather
selected and corrupted group

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:31.900
that maybe doesn't reveal a whole lot

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:33.660
about pre-theoretical intuitions

00:30:33.660 --> 00:30:36.316
because philosophers have
thought about this a lot.

00:30:36.316 --> 00:30:38.800
Still interesting data.

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:40.417
There was, among scientists,

00:30:40.417 --> 00:30:44.360
I don't know of an exact
equivalent of the PhilPapers survey

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:48.590
for surveying psychologists,
neuroscientists,

00:30:48.590 --> 00:30:51.060
AI researchers in general but I do know,

00:30:51.060 --> 00:30:55.750
there was one survey
back in 2018 of attendees

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:58.960
at the Association for the
Scientific Study of Consciousness

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:03.960
where it's majority
neuroscientists and psychologists

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:07.580
with again 60% saying some
kind of explanatory gap

00:31:07.580 --> 00:31:11.880
on explaining functional
behavioral properties of things.

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:13.010
There's a gap to consciousness.

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:14.190
Again this group is selected

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:16.475
for being interested in consciousness.

00:31:16.475 --> 00:31:20.390
So by no means a pure sample
of pre-theoretical intuitions

00:31:20.390 --> 00:31:22.741
but interesting data all the same.

00:31:22.741 --> 00:31:23.657
There's obviously room

00:31:23.657 --> 00:31:26.300
for a lot more experimental work there

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:27.700
so I'm really trying to encourage

00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:30.620
this kind of experimental
philosophy and psychology

00:31:30.620 --> 00:31:33.990
of intuitions about consciousness.

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:37.560
So I want to say a bit
about potential solutions

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:39.659
to the problems.

00:31:39.659 --> 00:31:42.320
Because you know this idea
of explaining our intuitions

00:31:42.320 --> 00:31:45.630
has been around for awhile
but there are at least some

00:31:47.065 --> 00:31:50.455
kind of quasi-standard approaches here.

00:31:50.455 --> 00:31:52.853
I mean this literature
is extremely fragmented.

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:55.110
I think at least until recently

00:31:55.110 --> 00:31:57.570
there was just a proposal
here and a proposal there

00:31:57.570 --> 00:32:00.510
that didn't really make
contact with each other

00:32:00.510 --> 00:32:03.300
but I think it's possible
to systematize them

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:05.810
and I've tried to do this a little bit

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:09.330
by looking at a few different factors

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:10.310
that might be generating

00:32:10.310 --> 00:32:13.137
these intuitions about
consciousness and again,

00:32:13.137 --> 00:32:15.970
these moves are available
equally to the illusionist

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:17.640
who thinks it's all an illusion

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:22.000
and the realist who
thinks all this is real.

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:23.903
One key very basic idea.

00:32:27.290 --> 00:32:29.210
Doug Hofstadter I think has argued

00:32:29.210 --> 00:32:32.160
for this kind of view at some
length and more recently,

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:34.920
Michael Graziano's theory
is a version of this.

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:36.840
Basically says that it all comes down

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:39.410
to certain internal self models.

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:42.450
We all have models of ourselves.

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:43.283
In particular,

00:32:43.283 --> 00:32:47.300
models of our own cognitive processes.

00:32:47.300 --> 00:32:50.220
And these models can greatly
distort the real thing.

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:53.050
Sometimes they greatly
simplify the real thing.

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:54.800
Sometimes they add to the real thing.

00:32:54.800 --> 00:32:57.070
They didn't exactly resemble,

00:32:57.070 --> 00:32:59.960
the didn't represent
the real thing perfectly

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:03.510
but these introspective
models will somehow explain

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:05.720
our sense that we're conscious

00:33:05.720 --> 00:33:08.066
and that there's a
problem of consciousness

00:33:08.066 --> 00:33:09.620
and that much I find quite plausible

00:33:09.620 --> 00:33:11.320
but now the question is how.

00:33:11.320 --> 00:33:16.320
What kind of introspective
models are going to do this?

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:20.910
Well here's a Escher
print that Hofstader uses

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:23.577
that somehow explain the idea that

00:33:23.577 --> 00:33:26.690
our wonky, self models
somehow lead to the existence

00:33:26.690 --> 00:33:29.670
of the problem represented
by the big hole at the center

00:33:29.670 --> 00:33:30.970
where nothing makes sense.

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:36.040
A related idea is this idea

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:38.233
often associated with David Armstrong.

00:33:39.800 --> 00:33:42.410
Of introspective opacity
and here the key idea is,

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:46.580
well we don't see that
consciousness is physical.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:48.100
Introspection is opaque

00:33:48.100 --> 00:33:50.170
as to the underlying mechanisms.

00:33:50.170 --> 00:33:51.220
It doesn't show us

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:53.860
the underlying mechanisms
of consciousness.

00:33:53.860 --> 00:33:56.260
We don't see the consciousness as physical

00:33:56.260 --> 00:33:57.550
and then we go on to infer,

00:33:57.550 --> 00:34:00.760
we go on to see it as non-physical

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:04.530
and this is meant to have
some analogs with this.

00:34:04.530 --> 00:34:06.750
This circus illusion
of the headless person.

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:09.040
We don't see their head.

00:34:09.040 --> 00:34:11.480
So we see them as having no head.

00:34:11.480 --> 00:34:15.700
Here's a picture the so-called
headless woman illusion

00:34:15.700 --> 00:34:19.210
which was I guess popular
in 19th century circuses

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:20.750
and there's just, you know
there's someone sitting there

00:34:20.750 --> 00:34:22.830
with her head behind a screen

00:34:22.830 --> 00:34:24.930
and I guess the idea is that
under the right conditions,

00:34:24.930 --> 00:34:26.407
at least for a moment you
look at this and it's like,

00:34:26.407 --> 00:34:28.447
"Oh my God, that person has no head."

00:34:29.330 --> 00:34:31.450
I don't know how much
the perceptual system

00:34:31.450 --> 00:34:33.310
really gets taken in by this

00:34:33.310 --> 00:34:35.980
but maybe there were circumstances

00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:37.500
where it looks a little bit like that.

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:39.420
I certainly, I mean I do think

00:34:39.420 --> 00:34:41.880
it takes special circumstances
to get this going.

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:44.777
I mean I can block out
the heads of a few of you

00:34:44.777 --> 00:34:45.850
and it doesn't suddenly look like

00:34:45.850 --> 00:34:50.254
there's a bunch of headless
people in the audience.

00:34:50.254 --> 00:34:51.310
So I think there's,

00:34:51.310 --> 00:34:52.740
you only actually make this inference

00:34:52.740 --> 00:34:55.153
under certain very special,

00:34:55.153 --> 00:34:57.570
under certain very special conditions.

00:34:57.570 --> 00:35:01.023
So I think that itself needs explaining.

00:35:02.290 --> 00:35:03.940
There's also further
questions like you know,

00:35:03.940 --> 00:35:06.600
why doesn't introspection of say belief

00:35:06.600 --> 00:35:09.060
and other mental states
generate the same gap.

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:12.020
Indeed, even for our awareness of say,

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:13.900
of computers and algorithms.

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:17.000
I know my iPhone is running some kind of,

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:20.120
some kind of computation under
the surface when I use this

00:35:20.120 --> 00:35:20.953
but I've got no idea.

00:35:20.953 --> 00:35:22.930
I mean for most of us, it's totally,

00:35:22.930 --> 00:35:23.830
who knows what's going on in there.

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:26.580
I guess it's some kind of silicon circuits

00:35:26.580 --> 00:35:29.410
but you know we've got
no awareness of that.

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:32.000
We've got no awareness
of the physical processes

00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:34.010
underneath, underneath all this

00:35:34.010 --> 00:35:36.020
but it doesn't mean that
we suddenly have some sense

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:37.240
that it's non-physical.

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:40.080
So I think this
explanation overgeneralizes

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:42.533
even though there may be something to it.

00:35:43.759 --> 00:35:46.660
One idea is that color experience

00:35:46.660 --> 00:35:48.510
and other kind of
experiences may attribute

00:35:48.510 --> 00:35:51.600
certain kinds of primitive
qualities to objects.

00:35:51.600 --> 00:35:55.380
So there's red, that
red exit sign and so on.

00:35:55.380 --> 00:35:57.150
What's actually going on in nature

00:35:57.150 --> 00:36:00.470
is some very complex
reflectance property up there

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:02.850
but we model it as this very simple,

00:36:02.850 --> 00:36:07.510
primitive feature of you
know, redness in the world.

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:10.570
It's a primitive quality
instantiated out there in the world

00:36:10.570 --> 00:36:12.810
and that makes maybe for
some very simple model

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:13.730
of things in the world,

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:16.140
is having these different
primitive colors.

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:19.673
Likewise for yellow and the like.

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:23.620
In some other work I have thought of this

00:36:23.620 --> 00:36:28.260
as modeling the external world
asa kind of a garden of Eden

00:36:28.260 --> 00:36:29.710
with all these primitive qualities

00:36:29.710 --> 00:36:33.790
exactly as they appear
in, to us in perception.

00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:36.450
That identic world could at least serve

00:36:36.450 --> 00:36:40.740
as a useful model of the
complex, non-identic world

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:41.573
that we live in.

00:36:41.573 --> 00:36:43.180
So maybe, then the thought is
maybe something like that's

00:36:43.180 --> 00:36:44.720
going on in the mind.

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:46.440
Maybe to model the mind,

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:49.400
we also attribute primitive
qualities to the mind,

00:36:49.400 --> 00:36:54.100
the same way we attribute
primitive qualities to the world.

00:36:54.100 --> 00:36:56.750
I'm not sure that gets
at the key issue though

00:36:56.750 --> 00:36:58.490
which is tied to our experience

00:36:58.490 --> 00:37:03.490
or our awareness of qualities
and I think to get to that,

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:05.110
you really need to go beyond this,

00:37:05.110 --> 00:37:08.690
to well a sense of being
related to these qualities

00:37:08.690 --> 00:37:10.630
in the world and being aware of them.

00:37:10.630 --> 00:37:13.349
And maybe in particular
our internal models

00:37:13.349 --> 00:37:16.370
give us a sense of being acquainted.

00:37:16.370 --> 00:37:18.510
Both with concrete qualities

00:37:18.510 --> 00:37:21.940
and with our awareness
of them which I think is,

00:37:21.940 --> 00:37:24.290
I think this is, they say I think

00:37:24.290 --> 00:37:26.260
is getting closer to
the heart of the matter.

00:37:26.260 --> 00:37:27.720
But as with all these explanations,

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:30.200
it always just keeps pushing
back the question a bit for me.

00:37:30.200 --> 00:37:34.010
Okay but how and why does that work?

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:36.330
So if I were to kind of
put all this together

00:37:36.330 --> 00:37:37.400
into a summary,

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:41.250
here's my summary of one possible approach

00:37:43.140 --> 00:37:46.300
of potential beginning
of a solution sketch

00:37:46.300 --> 00:37:47.133
to the meta-problem.

00:37:47.133 --> 00:37:50.991
Why do we make, why do we have this sense

00:37:50.991 --> 00:37:52.990
there's a problem of consciousness?

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:54.670
One thing I like by the way
about this kind of summary

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:55.790
is you could either realize this,

00:37:55.790 --> 00:37:59.370
read this in a realist
tone of voice about,

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:00.300
from the point of view
of someone who thinks

00:38:00.300 --> 00:38:02.910
consciousness is real and
these are the mechanisms

00:38:02.910 --> 00:38:05.270
or in an illusionist tone of voice

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:06.760
where this is an account of the illusion.

00:38:06.760 --> 00:38:08.450
I think the kind of
explanation I'm giving here

00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:09.943
is neutral in that.

00:38:10.822 --> 00:38:12.902
You just say okay we
have introspective models

00:38:12.902 --> 00:38:17.030
deploying introspective
concepts of our internal states

00:38:17.030 --> 00:38:20.470
that are largely independent
of our physical concepts.

00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:23.070
These concepts are introspectively opaque,

00:38:23.070 --> 00:38:26.045
not revealing any of the
underlying mechanisms,

00:38:26.045 --> 00:38:30.190
our perceptual models,
perceptually attribute

00:38:30.190 --> 00:38:33.066
primitive perceptual
qualities to the world

00:38:33.066 --> 00:38:35.220
and our introspective models

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:39.310
attribute primitive mental
relations to these qualities.

00:38:39.310 --> 00:38:42.560
These models produce the
sense of acquaintance

00:38:42.560 --> 00:38:46.460
both with those qualities
and with our awareness

00:38:46.460 --> 00:38:48.390
of those qualities.

00:38:48.390 --> 00:38:50.280
I'm inclined to find something like this

00:38:50.280 --> 00:38:52.640
somewhat plausible as an account of

00:38:52.640 --> 00:38:55.821
what's going into the intuitions.

00:38:55.821 --> 00:38:57.090
At the same time it feels to be more like,

00:38:57.090 --> 00:38:58.807
less like an explanation of the intuitions

00:38:58.807 --> 00:39:01.130
and more like a kind of fleshing out

00:39:01.130 --> 00:39:02.660
of what the intuitions are.

00:39:02.660 --> 00:39:04.580
What I'd really like to
see is some kind of story

00:39:04.580 --> 00:39:09.130
of maybe some kind of algorithmic
basis for the intuitions.

00:39:09.130 --> 00:39:10.960
Where things went this way and where,

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:13.040
if you actually built that algorithm

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:17.510
you would see it displaying
the relevant intuitions.

00:39:17.510 --> 00:39:20.220
I mean one way we can test solutions

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:23.170
to the meta problem is by
trying to formulate them,

00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:25.200
maybe in some kind of algorithmic terms,

00:39:25.200 --> 00:39:28.670
test those explanations
with psychological studies

00:39:28.670 --> 00:39:31.200
and computational models.

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:32.480
I mean the only thing in this direction

00:39:32.480 --> 00:39:35.600
I really know so far is
something by Luke Muehlhauser

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:37.010
and Back Schlergeras

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:39.930
where they took some fairly simple ideas

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:40.900
about the meta problem,

00:39:40.900 --> 00:39:45.900
that the philosopher Francois
Kammerer and I had put forward

00:39:46.340 --> 00:39:47.810
and they tried to build in those ideas

00:39:47.810 --> 00:39:49.690
into a little software agent

00:39:49.690 --> 00:39:51.510
which they gave a bunch of axioms

00:39:51.510 --> 00:39:53.500
and the ability to derive conclusions

00:39:53.500 --> 00:39:56.660
from those axioms and
a few basic principles

00:39:56.660 --> 00:39:58.760
about consciousness as the axioms.

00:39:58.760 --> 00:40:00.480
And they actually found it produced

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:02.797
little theorems, little reports like,

00:40:02.797 --> 00:40:05.497
"Yeah consciousness, my experience of red

00:40:05.497 --> 00:40:09.940
"distinct from any brain
process and so on."

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:12.270
Okay which is promising and interesting

00:40:12.270 --> 00:40:15.550
although I think they,
it's very much a toy model

00:40:15.550 --> 00:40:19.773
and they do build quite
a lot into this model.

00:40:20.680 --> 00:40:21.630
Interesting question,

00:40:21.630 --> 00:40:26.170
if a machine issues the same
sort of problem reports as us,

00:40:26.170 --> 00:40:28.260
and caused by similar mechanisms

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:30.070
that we think are present in us,

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:32.530
should we take that actually as evidence

00:40:32.530 --> 00:40:35.280
that the machine is conscious?

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:37.560
Here a few people have suggested

00:40:37.560 --> 00:40:41.440
that you could actually
potentially use problem reports

00:40:41.440 --> 00:40:43.990
as a sort of Turing test
for machine consciousness.

00:40:43.990 --> 00:40:47.642
Aaron Sloman, Susan
Schneider, Ed Turner have said

00:40:47.642 --> 00:40:49.960
you know one way to test
whether a machine is conscious

00:40:49.960 --> 00:40:52.470
is to see whether it is
possible to mount consciousness.

00:40:52.470 --> 00:40:53.787
If it says you know,

00:40:53.787 --> 00:40:58.077
"Well I know I'm just a
bunch of silicon chips

00:40:58.077 --> 00:41:01.710
"but I feel subjectively
like so much more."

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:03.070
Then is that some kind of sign

00:41:03.070 --> 00:41:06.410
that it might actually be conscious?

00:41:06.410 --> 00:41:07.730
On the other hand you might say,

00:41:07.730 --> 00:41:09.810
the machine say, well Dan
why do I need to go through?

00:41:09.810 --> 00:41:12.530
Why do I need to be so
theoretically confused

00:41:12.530 --> 00:41:13.977
as those damn humans.

00:41:15.025 --> 00:41:16.810
It's like in the regular
Turing test the poor machine

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:21.460
has to drag itself down to
talk like a dumb human being.

00:41:21.460 --> 00:41:24.300
Here's the poor sad machine studying

00:41:24.300 --> 00:41:27.140
the talk like a human test
book, test preparation book.

00:41:27.140 --> 00:41:28.390
Likewise for the meta problem,

00:41:28.390 --> 00:41:30.140
it's like okay why do
I really need to have

00:41:30.140 --> 00:41:32.440
confused intuitions about consciousness

00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:34.320
as the humans did do?

00:41:34.320 --> 00:41:37.260
But if it did display
those intuitions though,

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:39.653
maybe that would be some evidence.

00:41:41.757 --> 00:41:42.590
I was gonna say something

00:41:42.590 --> 00:41:44.820
about impact on theories of consciousness

00:41:44.820 --> 00:41:47.120
although maybe I think I'll skip over this

00:41:47.120 --> 00:41:48.810
for reasons of time.

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:51.100
Here the idea is from
a realist perspective,

00:41:51.100 --> 00:41:53.090
a solution to the meta-problem

00:41:53.090 --> 00:41:54.700
and a solution to the hard problem

00:41:54.700 --> 00:41:56.150
might be closely connected

00:41:56.150 --> 00:41:58.420
and we could use this as a
kind of test for theories

00:41:58.420 --> 00:41:59.253
of consciousness.

00:41:59.253 --> 00:42:01.180
See whatever explains consciousness

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:02.620
should also partly explain

00:42:02.620 --> 00:42:04.830
our judgements about consciousness.

00:42:04.830 --> 00:42:06.330
And here the idea is it would be strange

00:42:06.330 --> 00:42:09.510
if the basis of consciousness
played no role at all

00:42:09.510 --> 00:42:11.830
in judgments about consciousness.

00:42:11.830 --> 00:42:13.870
And here I go on to argue

00:42:13.870 --> 00:42:16.582
that some theories of consciousness

00:42:16.582 --> 00:42:19.060
don't seem to pass this test.

00:42:19.060 --> 00:42:22.609
That the mechanisms of
consciousness explain the judgments

00:42:22.609 --> 00:42:24.140
about consciousness.

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:25.220
I mean I argue for example

00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:28.990
that Tononi's integrated
information theory has trouble

00:42:28.990 --> 00:42:31.210
meeting this, meeting this challenge

00:42:31.210 --> 00:42:34.890
but maybe I'll skip over
that for reasons of time.

00:42:34.890 --> 00:42:36.470
So just to conclude I'll,

00:42:36.470 --> 00:42:38.510
and just by saying
something a little bit about

00:42:38.510 --> 00:42:40.910
this underlying issue between the realist

00:42:40.910 --> 00:42:43.210
and the illusionist about consciousness.

00:42:43.210 --> 00:42:45.900
Some of the illusionist says
consciousness doesn't exist,

00:42:45.900 --> 00:42:47.390
we just think it does.

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:51.533
A solution to the meta-problem
dissolves the hard problem.

00:42:52.680 --> 00:42:54.810
Strong illusionism says
phenomenal consciousness

00:42:54.810 --> 00:42:56.163
does not exist at all.

00:42:57.325 --> 00:42:59.600
You know this is a very
attractively austere view

00:42:59.600 --> 00:43:01.620
of consciousness.

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:06.500
Its main problem is it seems
somehow impossible to believe.

00:43:06.500 --> 00:43:09.460
Here's one way to put the
argument against it as a,

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:10.680
just a flat footed argument.

00:43:10.680 --> 00:43:13.000
It's like people sometimes feel pain.

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:15.920
If strong illusionism is
true, no one feels pain.

00:43:15.920 --> 00:43:18.130
Therefore strong illusionism is false.

00:43:18.130 --> 00:43:19.160
Well here the underlying idea is,

00:43:19.160 --> 00:43:20.520
well it's just a datam

00:43:20.520 --> 00:43:23.260
that we have certain kinds
of conscious experiences

00:43:23.260 --> 00:43:26.550
like feeling pain, experiencing red.

00:43:26.550 --> 00:43:28.920
You might put this in some
neutral form, it's like,

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:30.090
well it's unbelievable,

00:43:30.090 --> 00:43:32.460
it's just impossible
to believe illusionism

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:35.970
because it's somehow unbelievable
that no one feels pain

00:43:35.970 --> 00:43:38.119
and that no one is conscious.

00:43:38.119 --> 00:43:39.580
So illusionism is unbelievable.

00:43:39.580 --> 00:43:41.170
Now the illusionist does themselves

00:43:41.170 --> 00:43:43.193
have an interesting reply here which is,

00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:48.723
my view predicts that
my view is unbelievable.

00:43:49.649 --> 00:43:50.950
You know it's just those models

00:43:50.950 --> 00:43:52.850
you have in your brain that forces you

00:43:52.850 --> 00:43:55.260
to think you're conscious
and that consciousness

00:43:55.260 --> 00:43:59.053
can't be an illusion but
it might still be wrong.

00:44:00.040 --> 00:44:01.890
So just to conclude then,

00:44:01.890 --> 00:44:04.175
when we're putting this
kind of illusionist,

00:44:04.175 --> 00:44:08.730
realist showdown as
between two different forms

00:44:08.730 --> 00:44:10.540
of absurdity.

00:44:10.540 --> 00:44:12.490
Two different kinds of
claims about absurdity.

00:44:12.490 --> 00:44:15.250
Here's the philosopher Galen Strawson

00:44:15.250 --> 00:44:17.687
saying illusionism is absurd.

00:44:17.687 --> 00:44:19.457
"There occurred in the 20th century

00:44:19.457 --> 00:44:23.417
"the most remarkable episode
in the whole history of ideas.

00:44:23.417 --> 00:44:26.027
"A number of thinkers denied the existence

00:44:26.027 --> 00:44:28.527
"of something we know
with certainty to exist,

00:44:28.527 --> 00:44:30.020
"namely consciousness."

00:44:30.020 --> 00:44:32.550
Okay so it's the most absurd
thing he's ever heard.

00:44:32.550 --> 00:44:35.630
Here on the other side is
the rationalist philosopher,

00:44:35.630 --> 00:44:38.080
Eliezer Yudkowsky.

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:43.080
Arguing that epiphenomenalism
is absurd and here he was,

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:46.230
this was something he wrote
engaging with some of my ideas

00:44:46.230 --> 00:44:48.160
in my book "The Conscious Mind"

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:50.307
where I talked about zombies.

00:44:50.307 --> 00:44:51.757
He said, "The Zombie
Argument may be a candidate

00:44:51.757 --> 00:44:55.563
"for the most deranged
idea in all of philosophy.

00:44:56.397 --> 00:44:57.507
"The causally closed cognitive system

00:44:57.507 --> 00:44:59.707
"of Chalmers' internal narrative

00:44:59.707 --> 00:45:02.587
"is malfunctioning in a
way that not by necessity,

00:45:02.587 --> 00:45:03.727
"but just in our universe,

00:45:03.727 --> 00:45:05.950
"miraculously happens to be correct."

00:45:05.950 --> 00:45:07.910
And here, this is getting
at an interesting idea

00:45:07.910 --> 00:45:10.848
which is if you're a
realist about consciousness,

00:45:10.848 --> 00:45:11.930
maybe not just an epiphenomenalist

00:45:11.930 --> 00:45:15.050
but a realist more generally,
somehow it looks like

00:45:15.050 --> 00:45:16.490
you can explain all those things we say

00:45:16.490 --> 00:45:18.370
without mentioning consciousness.

00:45:18.370 --> 00:45:19.970
So something the fact
that the things we say

00:45:19.970 --> 00:45:21.510
are actually true ends up being

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:24.350
kind of a remarkable coincidence.

00:45:24.350 --> 00:45:27.780
So realism ends up being absurd too.

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:29.350
There is middle ground here of course

00:45:29.350 --> 00:45:30.930
between those extreme views.

00:45:30.930 --> 00:45:33.770
I think it tends to slide
back to the same problems.

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:37.560
Other forms of illusionism don't
help with the hard problem.

00:45:37.560 --> 00:45:40.190
Other forms of realism are still subject

00:45:40.190 --> 00:45:41.960
to the miraculous correctness

00:45:41.960 --> 00:45:46.380
or the idea that really
true by coincidence.

00:45:46.380 --> 00:45:50.060
So why I think to get
beyond absurdity here,

00:45:50.060 --> 00:45:52.650
I think there's challenges for both sides.

00:45:52.650 --> 00:45:55.038
The illusionist needs to explain

00:45:55.038 --> 00:45:58.903
how having a mind can be somehow like this

00:45:58.903 --> 00:46:01.080
even if it's not the way,

00:46:01.080 --> 00:46:03.640
if it's not completely
the way that it seems.

00:46:03.640 --> 00:46:05.480
The realist needs to explain,

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:08.310
to avoid this coincidence worry
the realist needs to explain

00:46:08.310 --> 00:46:10.300
how meta-problem processes

00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:13.460
are essentially grounded in consciousness

00:46:13.460 --> 00:46:14.430
even if it's possible

00:46:14.430 --> 00:46:18.370
for them to occur without consciousness.

00:46:18.370 --> 00:46:19.510
But either way I think.

00:46:19.510 --> 00:46:22.470
I mean if we can get a
solution to the meta-problem

00:46:22.470 --> 00:46:24.160
that meets these ambitions,

00:46:24.160 --> 00:46:27.190
that may just possibly lead to a solution

00:46:27.190 --> 00:46:29.690
to the hard problem of consciousness.

00:46:29.690 --> 00:46:32.150
In the meantime I think the meta-problem

00:46:32.150 --> 00:46:34.750
is a potentially
tractable research project

00:46:34.750 --> 00:46:36.665
for everyone, thank you.

00:46:36.665 --> 00:46:39.665
(audience applauds)

00:46:43.342 --> 00:46:45.881
- [Lori] Two minutes to spare.

00:46:45.881 --> 00:46:47.500
- Two to spare, very good.
- Well done.

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:50.660
Well now is the time for Q and A.

00:46:50.660 --> 00:46:52.657
Moderated by Professor Shull.

00:46:53.551 --> 00:46:54.384
- We have 10 to 15 minutes

00:46:54.384 --> 00:46:56.760
for questions for Professor Chalmers,

00:46:56.760 --> 00:46:58.270
after which he will be visiting

00:46:58.270 --> 00:47:01.090
the metaphysics meets
cognitive science course.

00:47:01.090 --> 00:47:03.783
Who has some questions
about consciousness?

00:47:09.030 --> 00:47:10.430
Who has some meta questions?

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:18.003
- I.
- Anyone, anyone?

00:47:19.207 --> 00:47:20.878
Sure.
- I'll start things off

00:47:20.878 --> 00:47:21.711
if I can.

00:47:22.895 --> 00:47:24.895
So in, oh yes thank you.

00:47:26.070 --> 00:47:29.470
In your slide where you
kind of summarize the view

00:47:29.470 --> 00:47:31.300
that you were developing.

00:47:31.300 --> 00:47:33.470
I actually just had a question
about the interpretation

00:47:33.470 --> 00:47:35.990
of that text and so I wanted
to ask you about that.

00:47:35.990 --> 00:47:37.490
Thanks for going back to that.

00:47:38.980 --> 00:47:42.237
Yes, so you say our perceptual models

00:47:42.237 --> 00:47:45.460
perceptually attribute
primitive perceptual qualities

00:47:45.460 --> 00:47:46.650
to the world.

00:47:46.650 --> 00:47:48.620
And I just didn't understand that phrase,

00:47:48.620 --> 00:47:50.115
this is legitimate.

00:47:50.115 --> 00:47:50.948
And just if you could
say a little bit more

00:47:50.948 --> 00:47:53.570
about what you mean by
perceptual attribution

00:47:53.570 --> 00:47:58.570
and primitive perceptual
qualities and then I actually then

00:47:58.710 --> 00:48:00.000
had a secondary question which is,

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:01.795
I don't really know what you mean

00:48:01.795 --> 00:48:03.091
by the sense of acquaintance.

00:48:03.091 --> 00:48:03.990
So I'm just wondering

00:48:03.990 --> 00:48:05.307
if you could elaborate
on that a little bit.

00:48:05.307 --> 00:48:06.770
- Okay so the perceptual models

00:48:06.770 --> 00:48:08.470
and the primitive perceptual qualities

00:48:08.470 --> 00:48:11.060
was meant to be a version
of you know, this thing.

00:48:11.060 --> 00:48:14.480
The, like the intuition's
ground learning color experience

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:15.830
that somehow yeah,

00:48:15.830 --> 00:48:18.840
in perception it's like
redness just seems to be this,

00:48:18.840 --> 00:48:20.950
kind of this primitive quality of objects.

00:48:20.950 --> 00:48:23.950
That redness is kind of
spread out over the exit sign.

00:48:23.950 --> 00:48:26.800
It seems to be this very distinctive,

00:48:26.800 --> 00:48:29.730
shiny quality out there.

00:48:29.730 --> 00:48:31.220
As far as perception is concerned,

00:48:31.220 --> 00:48:33.470
it seems to be basically
a primitive thing.

00:48:33.470 --> 00:48:36.010
There's red and there's and there's green

00:48:36.010 --> 00:48:37.890
and maybe there's amounts of,

00:48:37.890 --> 00:48:40.820
there's amounts of redness
and amounts of blueness

00:48:40.820 --> 00:48:43.060
that we can decompose phenomenologically

00:48:43.060 --> 00:48:46.250
but it certainly doesn't
seem to be a complex property

00:48:46.250 --> 00:48:48.013
or a reducible property.

00:48:48.013 --> 00:48:48.846
So many people have made this point

00:48:48.846 --> 00:48:50.530
about the phenomenology of color.

00:48:50.530 --> 00:48:52.920
That in seeing a color, we seem to be,

00:48:52.920 --> 00:48:54.460
the world seems to be populated

00:48:54.460 --> 00:48:57.844
by these special primitive qualities

00:48:57.844 --> 00:49:00.300
and I think something
similar might go for a pitch,

00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:02.490
you can argue about space.

00:49:02.490 --> 00:49:04.881
But at least for a color,

00:49:04.881 --> 00:49:08.130
are you uncomfortable with that much?

00:49:08.130 --> 00:49:08.963
- [Woman] No that's I see.

00:49:08.963 --> 00:49:11.050
You heard what I was talking about with,

00:49:11.050 --> 00:49:13.240
I mean there's also say the way that Brian

00:49:13.240 --> 00:49:14.380
would describe a perception

00:49:14.380 --> 00:49:16.240
and it would be a little bit different.

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:17.662
So I'm trying to kind of
put this together with.

00:49:17.662 --> 00:49:19.406
- Yeah, so this is very much from

00:49:19.406 --> 00:49:20.600
the phenomenological perspective.

00:49:20.600 --> 00:49:23.540
This is how, this is kind
of how perception seems.

00:49:23.540 --> 00:49:26.880
It seems to attribute
these primitive qualities

00:49:26.880 --> 00:49:28.180
to things in the world.

00:49:28.180 --> 00:49:30.680
- [Woman] And then the point is again,

00:49:30.680 --> 00:49:31.750
I find it very difficult
to try to talk about this

00:49:31.750 --> 00:49:33.010
in a way that it's like,

00:49:33.010 --> 00:49:35.052
I get into it a little bit

00:49:35.052 --> 00:49:36.540
but then how to express it
in language is very hard.

00:49:36.540 --> 00:49:38.000
So what do you mean?

00:49:38.000 --> 00:49:41.118
What is acquaintance
here and what does it do?

00:49:41.118 --> 00:49:42.880
- Yeah it's a good question.

00:49:42.880 --> 00:49:44.910
I think the basic idea is we seem to stand

00:49:44.910 --> 00:49:47.670
in a particular direct
epistemological relation

00:49:47.670 --> 00:49:48.860
to these properties.

00:49:48.860 --> 00:49:51.910
Both the properties in the
world, the redness out there.

00:49:51.910 --> 00:49:53.560
I think phenomenologically it seems

00:49:53.560 --> 00:49:55.097
that there's some redness out there

00:49:55.097 --> 00:49:59.450
and it seems that I'm in
very direct contact with it.

00:49:59.450 --> 00:50:01.990
I mean exactly what nature
that contact has is,

00:50:01.990 --> 00:50:04.630
you know you can argue
about that but you know,

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:06.240
people have the catapult theories

00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:09.320
and the intermission
theories and I don't know.

00:50:09.320 --> 00:50:11.980
But also I think even more,

00:50:11.980 --> 00:50:13.690
when it comes to consciousness itself

00:50:13.690 --> 00:50:17.520
to my awareness of that
redness, my experience.

00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:18.770
There's this idea that we have

00:50:18.770 --> 00:50:20.827
some kind of direct acquaintance with,

00:50:20.827 --> 00:50:23.340
with what it's like to be us.

00:50:23.340 --> 00:50:26.850
Maybe more primitive than
any conceptual grasp.

00:50:26.850 --> 00:50:28.802
It's just that those
properties are somehow

00:50:28.802 --> 00:50:30.710
phenomenologically it feels like,

00:50:30.710 --> 00:50:32.850
you know phenomenal properties
are just there for us.

00:50:32.850 --> 00:50:35.090
When Mary sees red for the first time,

00:50:35.090 --> 00:50:38.530
she becomes acquainted with
a certain kind of experience

00:50:38.530 --> 00:50:39.840
that she hadn't had before.

00:50:39.840 --> 00:50:42.980
And maybe that intuition is
wrong but I think it, it is a.

00:50:42.980 --> 00:50:47.620
I don't know that it's a direct
phenomenological intuition.

00:50:47.620 --> 00:50:49.600
I mean acquaintance is
kind of a technical term.

00:50:49.600 --> 00:50:51.690
This is already getting
a little bit theoretical.

00:50:51.690 --> 00:50:53.327
So think of it maybe more

00:50:53.327 --> 00:50:55.100
as something which just lies behind

00:50:55.100 --> 00:50:58.330
some of our more surface intuitions

00:50:58.330 --> 00:51:01.220
as a potentially unifying
explainer of them,

00:51:01.220 --> 00:51:04.218
this sense of acquaintance.
- Thank you.

00:51:04.218 --> 00:51:05.882
- [Lori] Oh there's a, I see a question.

00:51:05.882 --> 00:51:08.392
I will follow on the class here.

00:51:08.392 --> 00:51:11.954
- [Woman] Yeah I know you yourself.

00:51:11.954 --> 00:51:15.782
- Thanks.
- Hello.

00:51:15.782 --> 00:51:18.380
I know you're not an illusionist yourself

00:51:18.380 --> 00:51:21.542
but I kind of wanted to clarify that idea.

00:51:21.542 --> 00:51:25.220
Normally with skepticism
there's kind of this gap

00:51:25.220 --> 00:51:29.283
between what is and what we perceive.

00:51:29.283 --> 00:51:31.390
So if I'm a skeptic, I
perceive a table here

00:51:31.390 --> 00:51:33.830
but how I do know that
there actually is a table,

00:51:33.830 --> 00:51:35.410
that there's nothing.

00:51:35.410 --> 00:51:36.640
With illusionism,

00:51:36.640 --> 00:51:37.770
how can there be a gap

00:51:37.770 --> 00:51:42.770
between kind of what we think
we think and what we think.

00:51:43.810 --> 00:51:46.100
To me, I'm kind of on
the, this is absurd camp

00:51:46.100 --> 00:51:49.050
but I think there's probably
a more charitable explanation.

00:51:50.262 --> 00:51:52.270
- Yeah, I mean certainly many people

00:51:52.270 --> 00:51:56.460
have accused the view of
being somehow contradictory

00:51:56.460 --> 00:51:58.848
or self undermining.

00:51:58.848 --> 00:52:00.640
I think one way to put it is here.

00:52:00.640 --> 00:52:02.550
That strong illusionism is contradictory

00:52:02.550 --> 00:52:04.470
because you might think
that the illusion itself,

00:52:04.470 --> 00:52:06.040
to undergo an illusion

00:52:06.040 --> 00:52:08.670
is itself a kind of conscious experience.

00:52:08.670 --> 00:52:11.770
So you're saying you're conscious.

00:52:11.770 --> 00:52:13.240
Yeah, well you're not actually conscious.

00:52:13.240 --> 00:52:14.690
You're merely,

00:52:14.690 --> 00:52:16.273
you merely have the
illusion of consciousness.

00:52:16.273 --> 00:52:17.870
Well if you understood that as requiring

00:52:17.870 --> 00:52:20.290
the conscious experience
that you're conscious

00:52:20.290 --> 00:52:22.743
then that would seem to be contradictory.

00:52:23.844 --> 00:52:24.750
So I think the illusionist

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:27.526
had better understand illusions here

00:52:27.526 --> 00:52:30.302
as something that doesn't
require consciousness.

00:52:30.302 --> 00:52:31.270
I mean maybe it could be
something analogous to say

00:52:31.270 --> 00:52:33.483
unconscious perception.

00:52:34.409 --> 00:52:36.257
But maybe another way to think about it is

00:52:36.257 --> 00:52:38.540
this is something that goes
on at the level of judgment.

00:52:38.540 --> 00:52:40.930
We've got things we
perceive to be the case

00:52:40.930 --> 00:52:43.880
and things we judge to
be the case and yeah,

00:52:43.880 --> 00:52:47.630
maybe on views judgment is a conscious act

00:52:47.630 --> 00:52:48.910
or very often a conscious act

00:52:48.910 --> 00:52:51.210
but I think the thought is
we could, there is a version.

00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:52.410
There's an analog of judgment,

00:52:52.410 --> 00:52:54.740
the kind of mental act of
taking something to be the case

00:52:54.740 --> 00:52:56.590
that needn't actually be conscious.

00:52:56.590 --> 00:52:59.830
So maybe think of this as
a non-conscious judgment

00:52:59.830 --> 00:53:01.840
that we are conscious.

00:53:01.840 --> 00:53:03.310
Even maybe an AI system.

00:53:03.310 --> 00:53:05.200
Think about an AI system that goes around

00:53:05.200 --> 00:53:07.820
building these weird
representations of itself

00:53:07.820 --> 00:53:09.120
as having these special properties.

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:12.330
No one's inclined to say
the AI system is conscious.

00:53:12.330 --> 00:53:15.052
But it seems to at the very
least be producing the reports

00:53:15.052 --> 00:53:17.049
that they're conscious.

00:53:17.049 --> 00:53:17.973
I mean at the very least,

00:53:17.973 --> 00:53:21.191
think of the judgment as
a disposition to report.

00:53:21.191 --> 00:53:22.988
Then at least we've escaped
the charge hopefully

00:53:22.988 --> 00:53:24.150
of contradiction.

00:53:24.150 --> 00:53:24.983
Does that help?

00:53:32.310 --> 00:53:36.093
- [Man] Thank you, just also
a purely clarifying question.

00:53:37.040 --> 00:53:39.730
Can you say a little bit more
about what the coincidence is

00:53:39.730 --> 00:53:42.550
that all realist and not
only epiphenomenalist

00:53:42.550 --> 00:53:45.600
are meant to have to explain?

00:53:45.600 --> 00:53:48.740
I didn't really understand
what the accusation is

00:53:48.740 --> 00:53:50.153
from the illusionist side.

00:53:51.230 --> 00:53:54.476
- Sorry, this was about
acquaintance specifically?

00:53:54.476 --> 00:53:55.443
- [Man] Sorry, the coincidence.

00:53:56.548 --> 00:53:57.381
- Coincidence, oh yeah you're right.

00:53:57.381 --> 00:54:00.358
I didn't really say
nearly enough about that.

00:54:00.358 --> 00:54:01.540
But I think, here's one idea.

00:54:01.540 --> 00:54:05.120
Just say there's an
algorithmic explanation

00:54:05.120 --> 00:54:09.750
of why we say the things
we say about consciousness

00:54:09.750 --> 00:54:12.230
but there's no algorithmic
explanation of consciousness.

00:54:12.230 --> 00:54:14.730
Consciousness is something
supra-algorithmic

00:54:14.730 --> 00:54:17.784
as many realists think.

00:54:17.784 --> 00:54:20.110
And then think that's at least
an uncomfortable explanation

00:54:20.110 --> 00:54:20.943
'cause it starts to look

00:54:20.943 --> 00:54:24.320
as if what our judgements
about consciousness

00:54:24.320 --> 00:54:27.670
are made almost independently
of consciousness itself.

00:54:27.670 --> 00:54:28.727
And if then you go on to say,

00:54:28.727 --> 00:54:30.667
"Well but actually there are

00:54:30.667 --> 00:54:31.817
"these properties of consciousness

00:54:31.817 --> 00:54:33.607
"over and above the algorithm.

00:54:33.607 --> 00:54:36.518
"And the algorithm is getting them right."

00:54:36.518 --> 00:54:37.351
You know when it says,

00:54:37.351 --> 00:54:38.367
"Yeah I'm having that red experience.

00:54:38.367 --> 00:54:40.751
"I'm having experience that is structured

00:54:40.751 --> 00:54:42.580
"in a certain way, that's all going on."

00:54:42.580 --> 00:54:43.587
Then it starts to look like,

00:54:43.587 --> 00:54:46.187
"Boy, it's really lucky

00:54:46.187 --> 00:54:48.730
"that we actually got these things right."

00:54:48.730 --> 00:54:50.520
'Cause it looks like well that
algorithm could've gone on

00:54:50.520 --> 00:54:51.870
without consciousness.

00:54:51.870 --> 00:54:53.560
It could've gone on with
different consciousness.

00:54:53.560 --> 00:54:55.780
Maybe the psychophysical laws,

00:54:55.780 --> 00:54:58.030
the laws connecting
algorithms and consciousness

00:54:58.030 --> 00:55:00.570
are such that at least most of the time

00:55:00.570 --> 00:55:01.403
that's gonna be right.

00:55:01.403 --> 00:55:02.877
But even then it starts to look like,

00:55:02.877 --> 00:55:04.667
"Boy lucky that we live in a world

00:55:04.667 --> 00:55:06.547
"where the psychophysical laws

00:55:06.547 --> 00:55:11.547
"connect up the algorithms
to consciousness that way."

00:55:11.642 --> 00:55:12.475
So it's something like as a thought

00:55:12.475 --> 00:55:14.575
and I don't intend it to be conclusive.

00:55:14.575 --> 00:55:16.580
I think there are lots
of things to say in reply

00:55:16.580 --> 00:55:18.420
and I've heard people say
interesting things in reply

00:55:18.420 --> 00:55:19.300
but I did feel that's,

00:55:19.300 --> 00:55:21.070
for me that's perhaps the most,

00:55:21.070 --> 00:55:24.640
actually the most forceful
objection to realism

00:55:24.640 --> 00:55:25.653
about consciousness.

00:55:30.636 --> 00:55:33.030
- [Man] Hi, I noticed that
you said something about

00:55:33.030 --> 00:55:35.540
how the problem reports
are kind of evidence.

00:55:35.540 --> 00:55:37.258
Or it seemed like you were implying

00:55:37.258 --> 00:55:38.228
that they're kind of evidence.

00:55:38.228 --> 00:55:39.061
Not only that consciousness exists

00:55:39.061 --> 00:55:41.530
but also that it has a tangible,

00:55:41.530 --> 00:55:44.300
noticeable effect on the
world that we perceive

00:55:44.300 --> 00:55:46.490
and I was wondering what
are your thoughts on like,

00:55:46.490 --> 00:55:51.033
arguments about how
evolutionary function could,

00:55:51.970 --> 00:55:55.280
like why would it be beneficial
for an organism to evolve

00:55:55.280 --> 00:55:58.300
to not only present the
information that it experiences

00:55:58.300 --> 00:56:00.520
to the conscious processes of the,

00:56:00.520 --> 00:56:02.420
if consciousness is a physical process.

00:56:02.420 --> 00:56:04.870
Why would it present it to
that in an organized way?

00:56:04.870 --> 00:56:07.140
And then why would it
listen for a response

00:56:07.140 --> 00:56:08.360
from the conscious process

00:56:08.360 --> 00:56:10.170
and then allow that to affect the world

00:56:10.170 --> 00:56:11.170
in an organized way?

00:56:12.375 --> 00:56:13.813
- So what would be the
evolutionary benefit of?

00:56:15.127 --> 00:56:15.960
- [Man] Like why would it be beneficial

00:56:15.960 --> 00:56:19.280
for an organism to evolve so that it

00:56:19.280 --> 00:56:22.060
not only responds to phenomena outside

00:56:22.060 --> 00:56:23.180
but it also delivers it

00:56:23.180 --> 00:56:25.470
to this mysterious
consciousness phenomenon

00:56:25.470 --> 00:56:26.333
and then listens?

00:56:27.307 --> 00:56:28.140
I'm not so much just asking this question,

00:56:28.140 --> 00:56:30.710
I'm just asking like what do
you think of those arguments?

00:56:30.710 --> 00:56:32.758
Do you think that they hold water?

00:56:32.758 --> 00:56:34.519
Do you think they limit it in some way?

00:56:34.519 --> 00:56:36.541
- I don't have any good story to tell

00:56:36.541 --> 00:56:39.866
about the evolutionary benefits
of consciousness itself.

00:56:39.866 --> 00:56:42.713
This is one of the deepest
mysteries in the field.

00:56:43.570 --> 00:56:46.556
Anything you might posit we
need consciousness for that,

00:56:46.556 --> 00:56:47.850
looks like in principle,

00:56:47.850 --> 00:56:49.060
it's hard to see why you couldn't do that

00:56:49.060 --> 00:56:50.283
without consciousness.

00:56:51.247 --> 00:56:52.196
On the other hand,

00:56:52.196 --> 00:56:53.146
where the meta-problem is concerned,

00:56:53.146 --> 00:56:54.300
maybe there's something
interesting we can say

00:56:54.300 --> 00:56:56.760
about why there are evolutionary benefits

00:56:56.760 --> 00:56:59.110
in having a model of consciousness

00:56:59.110 --> 00:57:01.550
that attributes these special properties

00:57:01.550 --> 00:57:02.960
of consciousness to you.

00:57:02.960 --> 00:57:06.452
I mean I think of this, just
start say with the color case.

00:57:06.452 --> 00:57:09.130
Why might it be useful to have perceptual,

00:57:09.130 --> 00:57:13.570
some kind of model of colors
as these primitive things

00:57:13.570 --> 00:57:15.150
in the world?

00:57:15.150 --> 00:57:17.170
Maybe in the model, and the model's got,

00:57:17.170 --> 00:57:20.280
it doesn't build in these
complex reflective properties

00:57:20.280 --> 00:57:21.270
that exist in reality.

00:57:21.270 --> 00:57:22.103
Well we don't need all that complexity

00:57:22.103 --> 00:57:24.157
just to make these distinctions.

00:57:24.157 --> 00:57:24.990
It just turns out well it's really useful

00:57:24.990 --> 00:57:28.510
to be able to you know, distinguish

00:57:28.510 --> 00:57:31.560
certain kinds of fruits from others

00:57:31.560 --> 00:57:35.056
and certain kinds of
physical objects from others

00:57:35.056 --> 00:57:36.350
and these are attributing
these primitive qualities

00:57:36.350 --> 00:57:38.440
is just a really good way to do that.

00:57:38.440 --> 00:57:39.750
Likewise when it comes to,

00:57:39.750 --> 00:57:41.220
I mean you can easily tell a story

00:57:41.220 --> 00:57:44.160
about why it's useful
for us to have a model

00:57:44.160 --> 00:57:45.930
of our own mind.

00:57:45.930 --> 00:57:48.777
And have a model indeed
of other people's minds

00:57:48.777 --> 00:57:51.530
and then at least with
a bit Of hand waving,

00:57:51.530 --> 00:57:53.955
you can tell some story about why it might

00:57:53.955 --> 00:57:56.899
actually be useful to say,

00:57:56.899 --> 00:57:57.895
"Oh whenever we're."

00:57:57.895 --> 00:57:59.350
Instead of saying we've got
these really complex causal,

00:57:59.350 --> 00:58:02.361
representational relations
to things in the world.

00:58:02.361 --> 00:58:04.132
It's like no, we have the
simple relation of consciousness

00:58:04.132 --> 00:58:06.660
to those qualities.

00:58:06.660 --> 00:58:09.110
Now I don't have a detailed
version of that story.

00:58:09.110 --> 00:58:11.090
People like Graziano and Nick Humphrey

00:58:11.090 --> 00:58:13.270
have tried to sketch versions
of stories like that.

00:58:13.270 --> 00:58:15.396
I think there's at least

00:58:15.396 --> 00:58:17.082
an interesting research program there

00:58:17.082 --> 00:58:19.070
of looking at the benefits of self models

00:58:19.070 --> 00:58:22.330
with certain properties
that may be for example,

00:58:22.330 --> 00:58:25.223
socially useful in
dealing with other people.

00:58:26.059 --> 00:58:27.420
You know we have to communicate

00:58:27.420 --> 00:58:29.060
our states of mind with each other.

00:58:29.060 --> 00:58:32.777
Having a kind of, yeah maybe
certain kinds of self models

00:58:32.777 --> 00:58:34.573
involving models of
consciousness can help.

00:58:41.890 --> 00:58:44.610
- [Woman] I was wondering
if you differentiate between

00:58:45.710 --> 00:58:49.420
consciousness related and
consciousness independent sources

00:58:49.420 --> 00:58:51.330
of problem intuitions.

00:58:51.330 --> 00:58:53.220
I was reading the paper
that talked about that

00:58:53.220 --> 00:58:56.340
and I was confused about
how any of these intuitions

00:58:56.340 --> 00:58:58.340
that presumably come
from our conscious mind

00:58:58.340 --> 00:59:00.793
could be truly independent
of consciousness.

00:59:02.282 --> 00:59:04.290
- Yeah, I think what I
actually say in the paper

00:59:04.290 --> 00:59:07.780
is I want them to be topic neutral.

00:59:07.780 --> 00:59:09.020
I don't want these explanations

00:59:09.020 --> 00:59:11.190
to mention consciousness anywhere.

00:59:11.190 --> 00:59:12.330
If they mention consciousness,

00:59:12.330 --> 00:59:14.801
then they're no longer gonna be neutral

00:59:14.801 --> 00:59:18.380
between the illusionist and the realist.

00:59:18.380 --> 00:59:21.153
But I do think it's true
that at least the realist

00:59:21.153 --> 00:59:22.267
has got to look at some of
the things I say here and say,

00:59:22.267 --> 00:59:25.560
"All those things are
consequences of consciousness."

00:59:25.560 --> 00:59:27.890
Why do we have these introspective models

00:59:27.890 --> 00:59:29.760
deploying introspective concepts?

00:59:29.760 --> 00:59:33.411
Because we're conscious and you know,

00:59:33.411 --> 00:59:34.796
we're acquainted with
consciousness and whatever.

00:59:34.796 --> 00:59:35.629
And that's what actually
gives us the models

00:59:35.629 --> 00:59:37.030
and the concepts.

00:59:37.030 --> 00:59:41.000
Why do our models perceptually
attribute these qualities?

00:59:41.000 --> 00:59:42.870
Because there really are these qualities,

00:59:42.870 --> 00:59:44.023
at least In the mind.

00:59:44.889 --> 00:59:47.022
We really acquainted with consciousness.

00:59:47.022 --> 00:59:47.855
So I think that's a realist can say

00:59:47.855 --> 00:59:49.750
that this topic-neutral explanation

00:59:49.750 --> 00:59:51.770
is in fact dependent on consciousness.

00:59:51.770 --> 00:59:55.750
On the other hand, the
illusionist I think can endorse,

00:59:55.750 --> 00:59:58.510
hopefully endorse this
without saying any of it

00:59:58.510 --> 01:00:01.177
is dependent on consciousness

01:00:01.177 --> 01:00:03.370
and we somehow come to have
these introspective models.

01:00:03.370 --> 01:00:06.060
Without consciousness we have
this sense of acquaintance.

01:00:06.060 --> 01:00:07.910
Without consciousness but
somehow all that gives us

01:00:07.910 --> 01:00:11.503
the sense that we're conscious.

01:00:12.341 --> 01:00:13.748
So I guess yeah, the rule is.

01:00:13.748 --> 01:00:14.581
I think the rule is not

01:00:14.581 --> 01:00:17.203
that this has to be consciousness
independent in the sense

01:00:17.203 --> 01:00:19.500
that it's not actually grounded
in conscious consciousness.

01:00:19.500 --> 01:00:21.220
But maybe it's meant to
be at least an explanation

01:00:21.220 --> 01:00:23.990
that one can grasp without,

01:00:23.990 --> 01:00:27.040
without it somehow immediately entailing

01:00:27.040 --> 01:00:29.660
or requiring the
existence of consciousness

01:00:29.660 --> 01:00:30.750
if that makes sense.

01:00:30.750 --> 01:00:32.320
But yeah there's probably
a few different notions

01:00:32.320 --> 01:00:34.930
of conscious dependence here

01:00:34.930 --> 01:00:37.333
that I could probably be more
careful about separating.

01:00:38.210 --> 01:00:39.740
- [Man] I think we have
time for one more question

01:00:39.740 --> 01:00:42.190
before David has to head
upstairs to the seminar.

01:00:43.063 --> 01:00:43.896
Ian.

01:00:46.400 --> 01:00:48.750
- [Ian] So I have a
question about emergentism.

01:00:49.700 --> 01:00:52.700
In the study of complex systems,
for example neuroscience,

01:00:52.700 --> 01:00:56.190
one of the common theme
is that you can never,

01:00:56.190 --> 01:00:58.500
you can never explain the property

01:00:58.500 --> 01:01:02.550
of complex collective systems
from the single constituents.

01:01:02.550 --> 01:01:05.346
For example, memory and learning.

01:01:05.346 --> 01:01:09.040
Those complex functions can only emerge

01:01:09.040 --> 01:01:13.230
from the complex interaction
or dynamics between the groups.

01:01:13.230 --> 01:01:14.410
So I will,

01:01:14.410 --> 01:01:19.380
so I will ask do you think
the way Mary sees color

01:01:19.380 --> 01:01:21.580
is one of those emergent properties

01:01:21.580 --> 01:01:26.580
that cannot be explained by
the simple, simple materialism?

01:01:27.440 --> 01:01:30.270
- Yeah, so I think it is fairly plausible

01:01:30.270 --> 01:01:35.270
that consciousness emerges
from brain processes somehow

01:01:35.700 --> 01:01:37.560
but I think this word
emergence kind of covers up

01:01:37.560 --> 01:01:39.720
a variety of sins.

01:01:39.720 --> 01:01:42.920
It's like sometimes I say it's a kind of,

01:01:42.920 --> 01:01:44.560
like a magic word that people use

01:01:44.560 --> 01:01:47.506
to make them feel better about
things they don't understand.

01:01:47.506 --> 01:01:50.723
It's like the key question is
how does it emerge and why?

01:01:51.680 --> 01:01:54.110
Philosophers sometimes
distinguish strong emergence

01:01:54.110 --> 01:01:55.050
from weak emergence.

01:01:55.050 --> 01:01:58.540
Strong emergence involves
something fundamentally new.

01:01:58.540 --> 01:02:00.490
Weak emergence is more
like what you get say,

01:02:00.490 --> 01:02:02.337
in nonlinear systems.

01:02:02.337 --> 01:02:06.110
The game of life, it follows
a few very simple rules

01:02:06.110 --> 01:02:06.943
at the bottom level.

01:02:06.943 --> 01:02:07.910
And at the higher levels,

01:02:07.910 --> 01:02:10.720
yeah some really surprising things happen.

01:02:10.720 --> 01:02:11.580
But in those cases,

01:02:11.580 --> 01:02:12.710
it looks like the surprising things,

01:02:12.710 --> 01:02:14.690
they're surprising but
they're not too surprising

01:02:14.690 --> 01:02:17.420
in the sense that if you
actually work through

01:02:17.420 --> 01:02:19.400
the low level processes carefully

01:02:19.400 --> 01:02:21.581
you can tell some pretty
straightforward story

01:02:21.581 --> 01:02:23.550
about how it is that all
those rules come together

01:02:23.550 --> 01:02:25.840
and they'll generate the
high level phenomena.

01:02:25.840 --> 01:02:27.263
Say if I don't know,

01:02:27.263 --> 01:02:30.663
life or behavior of
complex cellular entities.

01:02:31.670 --> 01:02:33.033
So that's weak emergence.

01:02:33.990 --> 01:02:34.990
And in those cases actually,

01:02:34.990 --> 01:02:37.780
but what needed explaining was
still something much closer

01:02:37.780 --> 01:02:38.940
to the easy problems.

01:02:38.940 --> 01:02:41.710
Some complicated functional
things that a system does

01:02:41.710 --> 01:02:45.500
and yeah surprisingly some
simple mechanisms can do that.

01:02:45.500 --> 01:02:48.040
Nothing like that seems to
work I think for consciousness.

01:02:48.040 --> 01:02:49.530
It doesn't seem to just be a matter of,

01:02:49.530 --> 01:02:51.470
oh my God it's somewhat surprising

01:02:51.470 --> 01:02:53.660
but in principle if you
work through it enough

01:02:53.660 --> 01:02:57.410
you can see how this
would, this would come out.

01:02:57.410 --> 01:02:59.700
I mean the gap seems of a
somewhat different character here.

01:02:59.700 --> 01:03:03.010
It just seems like a
different kind of thing

01:03:03.010 --> 01:03:03.960
you're trying to explain.

01:03:03.960 --> 01:03:06.650
Not one of those objective
functions and because of that,

01:03:06.650 --> 01:03:08.840
you know, the gap seems
to be much more principled

01:03:08.840 --> 01:03:10.820
and this leads to it being a case

01:03:10.820 --> 01:03:14.670
of what philosophers call
strong emergence and yeah,

01:03:14.670 --> 01:03:16.887
if it's strong emergence
then the question is,

01:03:16.887 --> 01:03:18.437
"Now with something fundamentally new

01:03:18.437 --> 01:03:21.780
"maybe involving fundamentally
new properties in nature."

01:03:21.780 --> 01:03:23.100
Now it starts to look like a version

01:03:23.100 --> 01:03:26.290
of what philosophers
call property dualism.

01:03:26.290 --> 01:03:28.410
Which I think is okay and
that's also a possibility

01:03:28.410 --> 01:03:31.410
but insofar as many people
want to use emergence

01:03:31.410 --> 01:03:33.370
to kind of support a materialist view,

01:03:33.370 --> 01:03:35.410
come on it's just another
emergent property.

01:03:35.410 --> 01:03:40.237
Like life or like the weather.

01:03:40.237 --> 01:03:45.080
That will only work if
it's weak emergence, not.

01:03:45.080 --> 01:03:47.260
If that's meant to support
a materialist production,

01:03:47.260 --> 01:03:48.760
that will only work if
it's weak emergence.

01:03:48.760 --> 01:03:50.440
But I think that's less plausible

01:03:50.440 --> 01:03:51.890
in the case of consciousness.

01:03:53.160 --> 01:03:55.600
- Okay everybody, we are
in an awkward position now

01:03:55.600 --> 01:03:58.760
because we have already applauded
for these excellent ideas

01:03:58.760 --> 01:03:59.593
that David just shared with us

01:03:59.593 --> 01:04:02.260
and it would be sort of
weird to just do that again.

01:04:02.260 --> 01:04:04.770
So let us go meta and applaud David

01:04:04.770 --> 01:04:07.320
for handling the questions
and the organization

01:04:07.320 --> 01:04:09.271
of these ideas into talk.

01:04:09.271 --> 01:04:10.845
(audience applauds)

01:04:10.845 --> 01:04:12.145
- I applaud your applause.

